# Revolutions That Made the Modern World: American, French, and Russian

> Three revolutions — America in 1776, France in 1789, Russia in 1917 —
> destroyed monarchy's divine pretensions and gave the modern world its
> vocabulary: liberty, equality, fraternity, rights, constitutions, and
> planned economies. Each fed on Enlightenment ideas and ended further
> than it began.

## 1. Ideas Before the Revolutions

The Renaissance and Reformation taught Europe to question authority; the
**Enlightenment** turned that habit on politics itself.

| Thinker | Key work | Idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| John Locke | Two Treatises of Government | Natural rights; consent of the governed |
| Montesquieu | The Spirit of the Laws | Separation of legislative, executive, judicial powers |
| Rousseau | The Social Contract | General will; sovereignty of the people |
| Voltaire | Candide, Letters | Freedom of speech and religion |

[definition]
The **social contract** holds that legitimate government rests on agreement
among the people — rulers who break it may lawfully be replaced. Locke
justified England's 1688 revolution; Jefferson and Rousseau armed two more.
[/definition]

## 2. The American Revolution (1776)

Britain's victory in the Seven Years War (1763) left debts Parliament taxed
its thirteen colonies to pay — **Stamp Act (1765)**, Townshend duties, the
**Boston Tea Party (1773)**. The cry "**no taxation without representation**"
turned a fiscal quarrel into revolution. Fighting began at Lexington and
Concord (1775); the **Declaration of Independence (4 July 1776)** asserted
inalienable rights; Saratoga (1777) brought France in; Cornwallis
surrendered at **Yorktown (1781)**; the Treaty of Paris (1783) recognised
the United States. The **Constitution of 1787** built federal government,
separation of powers, and checks and balances — Washington first President —
and the first ten amendments, the **Bill of Rights (1791)**, completed the
template the world would copy.

[info title="The first modern republic"]
A written constitution, a bill of rights, an elected president, and federal
division of powers: the American formula adapted by France, Latin America,
and eventually India.
[/info]

## 3. The French Revolution: Causes

France in 1789 was a state bankrupted by wars — including aid to America —
facing bad harvests, with taxes falling on those least able to pay.

| Estate | Who | Privilege |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First | c. 100,000 clergy | Tithe; no taille |
| Second | c. 400,000 nobles | Seigneurial dues and exemptions |
| Third | 98% of the nation | Paid everything, owned grievances |

@chart donut title="Population of France by estate, 1789 (percent)"
labels:
First Estate
Second Estate
Third Estate

data:
0.5
