@/chart

## 4. The French Revolution: Course

- **5 May 1789:** Louis XVI summons the **Estates-General** for the first
  time since 1614; the Third Estate proclaims itself the **National
  Assembly** (17 June), sealing the **Tennis Court Oath** (20 June).
- **14 July 1789:** the **Bastille** falls; the Great Fear sweeps the
  countryside; feudal dues are abolished (4 August).
- **26 August 1789:** the **Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the
  Citizen** — liberty, property, equality before the law.
- **1791:** constitutional monarchy; **1792:** war with Austria and
  Prussia; the **Republic** is proclaimed and **Louis XVI guillotined
  (21 January 1793)**.
- **1793-94:** the radical **Jacobins** — Robespierre, Danton, Marat — rule
  through the Committee of Public Safety: the **Reign of Terror** sends
  some 17,000 to the guillotine, Robespierre included (**Thermidor, July
  1794**).
- **1795-99:** the corrupt Directory ends when **Napoleon Bonaparte** seizes
  power on **18 Brumaire (9 November 1799)**.

## 5. Napoleon and the Legacy

Napoleon crowned himself emperor (1804) and spread the **Code Napoleon** —
equality before the law, property rights, secular administration — across
Europe by conquest, from Austerlitz (1805) through the Russian disaster
(1812) to **Waterloo (1815)**.

| Legacy | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Rights | Liberty-equality-fraternity as political standard |
| Nationalism | Conscription and occupation awakened European nations |
| Metric system | Standardised in 1795, now global |
| Law | The Code Napoleon, model for civil codes worldwide |
| Land | Peasant purchases of Church lands were never undone |

## 6. The Russian Revolution: Causes and 1905

Autocracy had no parliament or free press, and a small but concentrated
industrial working class. Defeat by Japan in **1905** cracked the regime:
on **Bloody Sunday** (January 1905) troops fired on Father Gapon's
petitioners outside the Winter Palace, bringing strikes and the Battleship
Potemkin mutiny. The October Manifesto granted a **Duma** — which Nicholas
II then hollowed out. **1905 became, in Lenin's phrase, the "dress
rehearsal" for 1917**.

## 7. 1917: February and October

@diagram tree id="russian-1917" layout="tree" aria="The two Russian revolutions of 1917 and their outcomes"
  Russia 1917 -> "February Revolution"
  Russia 1917 -> "October Revolution"
  February Revolution -> "Tsar Nicholas II abdicates"
  February Revolution -> "Provisional Government under Lvov, then Kerensky"
  October Revolution -> "Bolsheviks seize power, 7-8 November"
  October Revolution -> "Peace, Land, Bread; all power to the Soviets"
@/diagram

In **February**, bread riots and mutinies at **Petrograd** forced
**Nicholas II to abdicate**; the Provisional Government continued the war
and lost the people. Lenin, returned in the sealed train, offered "**Peace,
Land, Bread**." On **7-8 November (old-style 25-26 October)** the
Bolsheviks, with Trotsky's Military Revolutionary Committee and the cruiser
**Aurora's** signal shot, took the **Winter Palace**. A three-year civil
war followed (1918-21): war communism, the **Red Army** Trotsky built,
foreign intervention, the crushing of the Kronstadt rising (1921), and the
**New Economic Policy** that restored markets to rebuild.

## 8. Stalin's Revolution and the Legacy

After Lenin's death (1924), **Stalin** outmanoeuvred rivals and replaced
the NEP with forced-march modernisation: the first **Five-Year Plan
(1928)** for steel, dams, and tractor factories, and **collectivisation**
of the farms — answered by "dekulakisation" and the famine of 1932-33
(the Ukrainian **Holodomor**, millions dead). The **Great Terror
(1936-38)** then purged party, army, and intelligentsia through show
trials and the Gulag.

[note]
The ledger: the **USSR (from December 1922)** industrialised a peasant
empire, defeated Nazi Germany, and launched the space age — at the cost of
tens of millions of lives. The state Stalin built dissolved in 1991, but
its Cold War division of the world shaped the whole twentieth century.
[/note]

## Key Takeaways

- Enlightenment ideas — Locke's rights, Montesquieu's separation,
  Rousseau's contract — supplied the vocabulary of all three revolutions.
- America (1776-91) built the constitutional republic; France (1789-99)
  radicalised through Terror to Napoleon; Russia (1917) replaced a class
  state with a one-party one.
- Their exports are everywhere: bills of rights, the metric system, the
  Code Napoleon, national self-determination, five-year planning.
